const turgorCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '8*(1 - x/10)', label: 'turgor pressure', color: '#15803d', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'external solute concentration (a.u.)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 9, label: 'turgor pressure (a.u.)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};

const osmosisCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The cell wall resists', note: 'Plant cells live inside a rigid box of cellulose. The box is the secret of plant life: it lets cells take on pressure.' },
    { math: '2 · Water follows solutes', note: 'The vacuole is full of dissolved solutes, so water tends to flow in by osmosis. In an animal cell this would burst the membrane.' },
    { math: '3 · Turgor pressure builds', note: 'Incoming water presses the membrane against the wall. That inward push from the wall is turgor pressure — the plant\'s internal skeleton.' },
    { math: '4 · Wilting is a pressure gauge', note: 'If the surroundings are saltier than the vacuole, water leaves, pressure drops, and the plant wilts — reversible, if you water it in time.' },
    { math: '5 · Turgor does work', note: 'Opening stomata, unfurling leaves and rapid movements (think touch-sensitive plants) are all turgor-pressure machines.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/botany/foundational/the-plant-cell">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plant cells are animal cells plus three inventions: a rigid <strong>cell wall</strong> of
    cellulose, green <strong>chloroplasts</strong> that capture sunlight, and a giant central
    <strong>vacuole</strong> that manages water and pressure. Those three additions explain almost
    everything distinctive about plants — why trees stand up without bones, why leaves are thin and
    flat, and why salt kills grass.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from solutes outside to turgor inside — how wilting works" widget="equationStep" config={osmosisCfg} />

  <h2>Turgor: the plant's hydraulic skeleton</h2>
  <p>
    Crunch a celery stick and you are hearing turgor pressure — water pushing living cells firm
    against their walls. The relationship is simple and dramatic: the saltier the surroundings, the
    less pressure the cell can hold. Salad wilts when dressed for exactly this reason, and the same
    physics moves stomata open each morning so photosynthesis can begin.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="turgor pressure falls as external solutes rise" widget="plotter" config={turgorCfg} />

  <h2>The chloroplast: a borrowed engine</h2>
  <p>
    Chloroplasts are the reason plants exist as we know them — and they were once free-living
    bacteria, engulfed by an ancestral cell and kept as internal solar panels. That endosymbiotic
    history is still visible: chloroplasts keep their own circular DNA and double membranes. Inside
    them, light is captured and used to fix carbon dioxide into sugar, the reaction that feeds
    nearly every ecosystem on Earth.
  </p>

  <h2>Meristems: growth without stopping</h2>
  <p>
    Unlike animals, plants grow their whole lives, and they do it from special zones called
    meristems — persistent pockets of stem cells at root and shoot tips. Because growth is confined
    to these zones, a plant can lose 90% of its body to a herbivore or a frost and simply grow it
    back. Modular, renewable, and wall-pressurised: the plant cell is a genuinely different solution
    to being alive.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Raven, Evert &amp; Eichhorn, <em>Biology of Plants</em>; OpenStax <em>Biology 2e</em>, plant structure units.</p>
</TopicPage>
